WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.960 --> 00:00:02.110
Film Courage: What is The Nutshell Technique?

00:00:02.110 --> 00:00:05.180
Jill Chamberlain, Script Consultant/Author/Writer:
The Nutshell Technique is a method that I

00:00:05.180 --> 00:00:14.139
created for my students and clients to address
this chronic problem I see that 99% of writers

00:00:14.139 --> 00:00:16.070
fail to tell a story.

00:00:16.070 --> 00:00:20.550
That instead the 99% situation.

00:00:20.550 --> 00:00:26.489
All I did is I drew up 8 essential elements
required to successfully tell a story, created

00:00:26.489 --> 00:00:34.880
this schematic (one-page schematic) and these
are not beats in time, these are elements

00:00:34.880 --> 00:00:37.350
that are connected that’s what makes it
different.

00:00:37.350 --> 00:00:41.019
This isn’t a strictly linear structure.

00:00:41.019 --> 00:00:45.960
If you look at the form (and folks can download
my form from my website Jill Chamberlain (dot)

00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:52.480
com) the key is the connect...see all of these
little arrows and things explain the connection

00:00:52.480 --> 00:00:56.870
between the boxes and that’s what makes
it different.

00:00:56.870 --> 00:01:02.960
I’m getting at the glue that is required
to tell a story, that is required to make

00:01:02.960 --> 00:01:04.890
sure that it’s not episodic.

00:01:04.890 --> 00:01:12.220
A story is not isolated events, there’s
a connection between them and this gets you

00:01:12.220 --> 00:01:17.540
visually (to see) and identify first of all
what are those elements?

00:01:17.540 --> 00:01:20.479
Do you have those 8 elements in your story.

00:01:20.479 --> 00:01:22.189
Then are they working together in conjunction.

00:01:22.189 --> 00:01:23.920
They need to work together.

00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:26.710
It’s about making choices actually.

00:01:26.710 --> 00:01:33.341
It’s about finding out what you are married
to because it forces you to get out of your

00:01:33.341 --> 00:01:36.829
head and on this piece of paper and it’s
not a lot of homework.

00:01:36.829 --> 00:01:42.290
It’s not like I’m asking you to write
a long beat sheet (multi-page beat sheet)

00:01:42.290 --> 00:01:43.290
to explain.

00:01:43.290 --> 00:01:48.290
It’s pretty brief (one page)…let’s see
if I can identify these 8 elements first of

00:01:48.290 --> 00:01:49.290
all.

00:01:49.290 --> 00:01:53.680
If you can’t you want to see visually how
they’re working together and if they’re

00:01:53.680 --> 00:01:56.759
not working you can see it right on the form.

00:01:56.759 --> 00:01:59.659
So it’s a way of finding out what am I married
to?

00:01:59.659 --> 00:02:05.950
I really love this premise about a guy who
wakes up and it’s the same day all over

00:02:05.950 --> 00:02:06.950
again.

00:02:06.950 --> 00:02:14.190
But it’s not working with this flaw that
I identified that he is naive.

00:02:14.190 --> 00:02:16.360
That flaw doesn’t work with this.

00:02:16.360 --> 00:02:17.700
Which one do you want to change?

00:02:17.700 --> 00:02:20.930
Do you want to change the flaw or change the
great premise?

00:02:20.930 --> 00:02:23.190
I’d probably stick with the great premise
right?

00:02:23.190 --> 00:02:27.750
But then I’d have to think what would be
the right character to put in that circumstance

00:02:27.750 --> 00:02:29.520
where they wake up and it’s the same day
again?

00:02:29.520 --> 00:02:32.290
Somebody who is naive, that wouldn’t really
work.

00:02:32.290 --> 00:02:34.750
That’s not a really good test for them.

00:02:34.750 --> 00:02:37.380
What about somebody who is really self-centric?

00:02:37.380 --> 00:02:38.420
Right?

00:02:38.420 --> 00:02:41.690
Now you can see the form “okay that works
much better.”

00:02:41.690 --> 00:02:46.130
This is going to be a much better test than
somebody who is self-centered and self-centric

00:02:46.130 --> 00:02:48.200
if they are repeating the same day again and
again.

00:02:48.200 --> 00:02:50.060
It’s going to get really boring right?

00:02:50.060 --> 00:02:56.180
It’s going to force them to start trying
different things and maybe finally change

00:02:56.180 --> 00:03:00.070
and find something better in themselves and
they realize that life isn’t really worth

00:03:00.070 --> 00:03:02.850
living if you’re just living it for yourself
everyday, right?

00:03:02.850 --> 00:03:08.890
So it’s going to take him all of that time
to figure out in this journey and this test

00:03:08.890 --> 00:03:14.030
you put him on in suddenly being caught in
a universe where he’s waking up and it’s

00:03:14.030 --> 00:03:19.950
the same day again to finally face this flaw
deep down in himself and to find a way to

00:03:19.950 --> 00:03:22.580
change and that’s how he breaks from the
cycle.

00:03:22.580 --> 00:03:27.980
Film Courage: And also play off of someone
who is more naive and who also has sort of

00:03:27.980 --> 00:03:30.110
a broader view of the world.

00:03:30.110 --> 00:03:32.450
Jill: Right, as a contrast to that character.

00:03:32.450 --> 00:03:35.570
So it’s a way of just isolating these elements
really simply.

00:03:35.570 --> 00:03:38.690
It’s very simple method really.

00:03:38.690 --> 00:03:43.580
Very simply you can see what’s working and
what’s not working right there on a piece

00:03:43.580 --> 00:03:44.580
of paper.

00:03:44.580 --> 00:03:49.950
It’s just a matter of kind of small adjustments
very often can make a huge difference between

00:03:49.950 --> 00:03:56.230
a huge story first of all not working (being
a situation and not feeling satisfying and

00:03:56.230 --> 00:03:57.460
one that is satisfying.

00:03:57.460 --> 00:04:04.900
It helps you find that unexpected and surprising
ending and satisfying way of doing things

00:04:04.900 --> 00:04:09.140
that you’re probably not going to find otherwise
if you’re just following a beat sheet method

00:04:09.140 --> 00:04:11.959
or if you’re just keep with a situation.

